'use strict';
/* =========================================================
   BUILT-IN QUESTION PACKS
   Written in the same plain-text format teachers paste in:
     Subject: ...   Topic: ...   Q: ...   A: ...
   The GCSE packs follow the AQA GCSE Combined Science: Trilogy
   topic list. They are "AQA-style" practice questions written for
   Showtime. They are not produced or endorsed by AQA.
   ========================================================= */
CGB.PACKS = {};

CGB.PACKS.biology = `Subject: Biology
Topic: Cell biology
Tier: 1
Q: Which type of cell division makes two genetically identical cells?
A: Mitosis
Q: Name two structures found in plant cells but not in animal cells.
A: Any two: cell wall, chloroplasts, permanent vacuole
Q: What is the function of ribosomes?
A: Protein synthesis (they are where proteins are made)
Tier: 2
Q: What is the function of the mitochondria?
A: The site of aerobic respiration, which releases energy for the cell
Q: Give the equation for magnification.
A: Magnification = size of image ÷ size of real object
Q: What type of cell has no nucleus and its DNA in a single loop?
A: A prokaryotic cell (bacteria)
Q: What is a stem cell?
A: An undifferentiated cell that can divide to make more cells like itself and can differentiate into other types of cell
Q: What is osmosis?
A: The diffusion of water from a dilute solution to a more concentrated solution through a partially permeable membrane
Tier: 3
Q: An image is 50 mm wide and the real object is 0.5 mm wide. What is the magnification?
A: ×100
Q: How is active transport different from diffusion?
A: It moves substances from a low to a high concentration (against the gradient) and needs energy from respiration

Subject: Biology
Topic: Organisation
Tier: 1
Q: Which blood vessels carry blood away from the heart?
A: Arteries
Q: Which part of the blood helps it to clot?
A: Platelets
Q: What is the test for starch, and what is a positive result?
A: Iodine solution turns from orange-brown to blue-black
Q: What is the loss of water vapour from the leaves of a plant called?
A: Transpiration
Tier: 2
Q: What does the enzyme amylase break down?
A: Starch into sugars (maltose)
Q: Where is the heart's natural pacemaker found?
A: In the right atrium
Q: What does the biuret test detect, and what is a positive result?
A: Protein. It turns from blue to purple (lilac)
Q: What do xylem vessels transport?
A: Water and mineral ions from the roots to the stems and leaves
Tier: 3
Q: Why do veins have valves?
A: To stop blood flowing backwards, because the blood is at low pressure
Q: Give two features of the alveoli that make gas exchange efficient.
A: Any two: large surface area, thin walls (short diffusion distance), good blood supply

Subject: Biology
Topic: Infection and response
Tier: 1
Q: What type of pathogen causes measles?
A: A virus
Q: Who discovered penicillin?
A: Alexander Fleming
Q: What type of pathogen causes salmonella food poisoning?
A: A bacterium
Tier: 2
Q: What type of pathogen causes malaria?
A: A protist
Q: Give two ways the body stops pathogens getting in (non-specific defences).
A: Any two: skin, hairs and mucus in the nose, cilia and mucus in the trachea and bronchi, stomach acid
Q: Which heart drug was first extracted from foxgloves?
A: Digitalis
Q: Give three ways white blood cells defend the body against pathogens.
A: Phagocytosis (engulfing pathogens), producing antibodies, producing antitoxins
Tier: 3
Q: Why can't antibiotics treat viral infections?
A: Antibiotics kill bacteria. Viruses live and reproduce inside cells, so antibiotics don't affect them
Q: How does a vaccine protect you from a disease?
A: It contains dead or inactive pathogens. White blood cells make antibodies, and memory cells give a fast response if you're infected later

Subject: Biology
Topic: Bioenergetics
Tier: 1
Q: What is produced by anaerobic respiration in muscle cells?
A: Lactic acid
Q: Give the word equation for aerobic respiration.
A: Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water
Q: Is photosynthesis an endothermic or an exothermic reaction?
A: Endothermic
Tier: 2
Q: Give the word equation for photosynthesis.
A: Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen
Q: Name two factors that limit the rate of photosynthesis.
A: Any two: light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration, temperature, amount of chlorophyll
Q: Give the word equation for anaerobic respiration in yeast.
A: Glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide
Q: Give two ways a plant uses the glucose made in photosynthesis.
A: Any two: respiration, making cellulose for cell walls, storing it as starch, making fats or oils, making amino acids for proteins
Tier: 3
Q: What is oxygen debt?
A: The extra oxygen needed after exercise to react with the lactic acid and remove it from cells

Subject: Biology
Topic: Homeostasis and response
Tier: 1
Q: Which gland produces insulin?
A: The pancreas
Q: Which hormone is produced by the testes?
A: Testosterone
Q: Which gland is often called the "master gland"?
A: The pituitary gland
Tier: 2
Q: What is Type 1 diabetes?
A: A disorder in which the pancreas fails to produce enough insulin
Q: How is Type 2 diabetes usually treated first?
A: A carbohydrate-controlled diet and an exercise regime
Q: Which hormone maintains the lining of the uterus?
A: Progesterone
Tier: 3
Q: What does glucagon do?
A: It makes the liver convert glycogen into glucose, raising blood glucose concentration
Q: Which hormone causes ovulation (release of an egg)?
A: LH (luteinising hormone)
Q: Which hormone controls metabolic rate and is regulated by negative feedback?
A: Thyroxine

Subject: Biology
Topic: Inheritance, variation and evolution
Tier: 1
Q: Which sex chromosomes does a human male have?
A: XY
Q: Who proposed the theory of evolution by natural selection?
A: Charles Darwin
Q: Which type of cell division makes gametes?
A: Meiosis
Q: Name a strain of bacteria that is resistant to antibiotics.
A: MRSA
Tier: 2
Q: How many chromosomes are in a human gamete?
A: 23
Q: What do we call a genotype with two different alleles?
A: Heterozygous
Q: Describe the structure of DNA.
A: A polymer made of two strands that form a double helix
Q: What is selective breeding?
A: Humans choosing plants or animals with useful characteristics and breeding them together over many generations
Q: What is a gene?
A: A small section of DNA on a chromosome that codes for a particular sequence of amino acids, to make a specific protein
Tier: 3
Q: Two heterozygous parents (Tt × Tt) have children. What proportion show the recessive phenotype?
A: 1 in 4 (25%)

Subject: Biology
Topic: Ecology
Tier: 1
Q: In a food chain, what do we call the organisms that make their own food?
A: Producers
Q: Name two processes in the water cycle.
A: Any two: evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation
Q: Which process in the carbon cycle removes carbon dioxide from the atmosphere?
A: Photosynthesis
Tier: 2
Q: Name two abiotic factors.
A: Any two: light intensity, temperature, moisture, soil pH or minerals, wind, carbon dioxide level, oxygen level
Q: Give two examples of biotic factors.
A: Any two: availability of food, new predators, new pathogens, competition between species
Q: What is a quadrat used for?
A: Sampling to estimate the abundance or distribution of organisms (usually plants) in an area
Q: What is biodiversity?
A: The variety of all the different species of organisms on Earth, or within an ecosystem
Q: What is an ecosystem?
A: The interaction of a community of living organisms with the non-living (abiotic) parts of their environment
Tier: 3
Q: What do decomposers do?
A: Break down dead organisms and waste, returning mineral ions to the soil and releasing carbon dioxide by respiration`;

CGB.PACKS.chemistry = `Subject: Chemistry
Topic: Atomic structure and the periodic table
Tier: 1
Q: What is the relative charge of an electron?
A: −1
Q: Who arranged the elements in a table and left gaps for elements not yet discovered?
A: Dmitri Mendeleev
Tier: 2
Q: How does reactivity change going down group 1?
A: It increases
Q: How does reactivity change going down group 7 (the halogens)?
A: It decreases
Q: An atom has 11 protons and a mass number of 23. How many neutrons does it have?
A: 12
Q: Give the relative masses of a proton, a neutron and an electron.
A: Proton 1, neutron 1, electron very small (about 1/2000)
Q: What is the electron arrangement of sodium (atomic number 11)?
A: 2,8,1
Q: What are isotopes?
A: Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons
Tier: 3
Q: Why are the noble gases unreactive?
A: They have a full outer shell of electrons (a stable arrangement)

Subject: Chemistry
Topic: Bonding, structure and properties
Tier: 1
Q: What type of bonding is in sodium chloride?
A: Ionic
Q: What type of bonding holds the atoms together in a methane molecule?
A: Covalent
Tier: 2
Q: What is the charge on the ion of a group 1 metal?
A: +1
Tier: 3
Q: Why can metals conduct electricity?
A: They have delocalised electrons that can move through the structure
Q: Why do ionic compounds conduct electricity when melted or dissolved, but not when solid?
A: When melted or dissolved the ions are free to move and carry the charge
Q: Why do small molecules such as water have low boiling points?
A: The forces between the molecules are weak, so little energy is needed to overcome them
Q: Why does diamond have a very high melting point?
A: It's a giant covalent structure, with lots of strong covalent bonds that need a lot of energy to break
Q: Why can graphite conduct electricity?
A: Each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds, so one electron from each atom is delocalised and free to move

Subject: Chemistry
Topic: Quantitative chemistry
Tier: 1
Q: What is the relative formula mass of water, H₂O? (H = 1, O = 16)
A: 18
Tier: 2
Q: What is the law of conservation of mass?
A: No atoms are lost or made in a reaction, so the total mass of the products equals the total mass of the reactants
Q: What is the relative formula mass of CO₂? (C = 12, O = 16)
A: 44
Q: What is the relative formula mass of NaOH? (Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1)
A: 40
Tier: 3
Q: What is the value of the Avogadro constant?
A: 6.02 × 10²³ (per mole)
Q: How many moles are in 88 g of carbon dioxide? (Mr = 44)
A: 2 moles
Q: 20 g of salt is dissolved to make 0.5 dm³ of solution. What is the concentration?
A: 40 g/dm³

Subject: Chemistry
Topic: Chemical changes
Tier: 1
Q: What is the pH of a neutral solution?
A: 7
Q: What are the products when a metal reacts with an acid?
A: A salt and hydrogen
Tier: 2
Q: Which ions make a solution acidic?
A: Hydrogen ions, H⁺
Q: Which ions make a solution alkaline?
A: Hydroxide ions, OH⁻
Q: What are the products when an acid reacts with a metal carbonate?
A: A salt, water and carbon dioxide
Q: Which metal is extracted by electrolysis because it is more reactive than carbon?
A: Aluminium
Q: Which salt is made when zinc reacts with hydrochloric acid?
A: Zinc chloride
Q: In electrolysis, which electrode do positive ions move to?
A: The cathode (the negative electrode)
Tier: 3
Q: What is oxidation in terms of electrons?
A: Loss of electrons
Q: Write the ionic equation for neutralisation.
A: H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O

Subject: Chemistry
Topic: Energy changes
Tier: 1
Q: Is combustion exothermic or endothermic?
A: Exothermic
Tier: 2
Q: What is an exothermic reaction?
A: One that transfers energy to the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings rises
Q: Give one everyday use of an exothermic reaction.
A: Any one: self-heating cans, hand warmers
Q: What is activation energy?
A: The minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react
Q: Give one example of an endothermic reaction.
A: Any one: thermal decomposition, citric acid with sodium hydrogencarbonate, photosynthesis
Tier: 3
Q: Is breaking chemical bonds exothermic or endothermic?
A: Endothermic (energy is needed to break bonds)

Subject: Chemistry
Topic: Rates and equilibrium
Tier: 1
Q: What does the symbol ⇌ mean in an equation?
A: The reaction is reversible
Q: Give two ways to increase the rate of a reaction.
A: Any two: increase temperature, increase concentration or pressure, increase surface area, add a catalyst
Tier: 2
Q: At equilibrium, what is true about the forward and reverse reactions?
A: They happen at exactly the same rate
Q: Give two ways to measure the rate of a reaction.
A: Any two: volume of gas given off over time, loss in mass over time, time for a cross to disappear as a precipitate forms
Tier: 3
Q: How does a catalyst speed up a reaction?
A: It provides a different pathway with a lower activation energy
Q: Why does increasing the temperature increase the rate of reaction?
A: Particles move faster, so they collide more often and more of the collisions have at least the activation energy

Subject: Chemistry
Topic: Organic chemistry
Tier: 1
Q: Crude oil is mainly a mixture of which compounds?
A: Hydrocarbons (mostly alkanes)
Q: Which process separates crude oil into fractions?
A: Fractional distillation
Tier: 2
Q: Name the first four alkanes.
A: Methane, ethane, propane, butane
Q: What is the test for an alkene, and what is the result?
A: Bromine water turns from orange to colourless
Q: What is cracking?
A: Breaking down large hydrocarbon molecules into smaller, more useful ones
Q: What are the products of the complete combustion of a hydrocarbon?
A: Carbon dioxide and water
Tier: 3
Q: What is the general formula for the alkanes?
A: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂

Subject: Chemistry
Topic: Chemical analysis
Tier: 1
Q: What is the test for hydrogen?
A: A lit splint gives a squeaky pop
Q: What is the test for carbon dioxide?
A: Limewater turns milky (cloudy)
Q: What is the test for oxygen?
A: A glowing splint relights
Tier: 2
Q: What does "pure" mean in chemistry?
A: A single element or compound, not mixed with any other substance
Q: What is the test for chlorine?
A: Damp litmus paper is bleached (turns white)
Q: What is a formulation?
A: A mixture designed as a useful product, with each component in a measured quantity
Q: How do you calculate an Rf value in chromatography?
A: Distance moved by the substance ÷ distance moved by the solvent
Tier: 3
Q: Why is the start line on chromatography paper drawn in pencil, not ink?
A: Pencil is insoluble, so it won't dissolve in the solvent and move up the paper with the spots

Subject: Chemistry
Topic: The atmosphere and resources
Tier: 1
Q: Name two greenhouse gases.
A: Any two: carbon dioxide, methane, water vapour
Q: Roughly what percentage of the atmosphere is nitrogen?
A: About 80% (78%)
Tier: 2
Q: What does "potable water" mean?
A: Water that is safe to drink
Q: Which toxic gas is made by the incomplete combustion of fuels?
A: Carbon monoxide
Q: Which gas from burning fuels containing sulfur causes acid rain?
A: Sulfur dioxide
Q: Where did most of the oxygen in today's atmosphere come from?
A: Photosynthesis by algae and plants
Q: What is desalination?
A: Removing dissolved salts from sea water, for example by distillation or reverse osmosis
Q: What does a life cycle assessment (LCA) measure?
A: The environmental impact of a product at each stage of its life
Tier: 3
Q: Why did the amount of carbon dioxide in the early atmosphere go down?
A: Algae and plants used it in photosynthesis, it dissolved in the oceans, and it was locked up in sedimentary rocks and fossil fuels
Q: How do greenhouse gases keep the Earth warm?
A: They let short-wavelength radiation from the Sun pass through, but absorb the long-wavelength radiation given off by the Earth and re-emit it, so less energy escapes into space`;

CGB.PACKS.physics = `Subject: Physics
Topic: Energy
Tier: 1
Q: What is the unit of power?
A: The watt (W)
Q: Name two renewable energy resources.
A: Any two: wind, solar, hydroelectricity, tides, waves, geothermal, bio-fuel
Tier: 2
Q: Give the equation for kinetic energy.
A: Kinetic energy = ½ × mass × speed²
Q: Give the equation for gravitational potential energy.
A: GPE = mass × gravitational field strength × height
Q: Give the equation for efficiency.
A: Useful output energy (or power) ÷ total input energy (or power)
Q: Give the equation linking power, energy and time.
A: Power = energy transferred ÷ time
Q: What is specific heat capacity?
A: The energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C
Tier: 3
Q: What is the kinetic energy of a 2 kg mass moving at 3 m/s?
A: 9 J
Q: How much gravitational potential energy does a 2 kg mass gain when lifted 5 m? (g = 9.8 N/kg)
A: 98 J

Subject: Physics
Topic: Electricity
Tier: 1
Q: What colour is the earth wire in a UK plug?
A: Green and yellow stripes
Q: What colour is the live wire in a UK plug?
A: Brown
Tier: 2
Q: How does current vary around a series circuit?
A: It is the same at every point
Q: Give the equation linking charge, current and time.
A: Charge = current × time
Q: What is the total resistance of a 4 Ω and a 6 Ω resistor in series?
A: 10 Ω
Q: What happens to the resistance of a thermistor as its temperature rises?
A: It decreases
Q: What are the frequency and voltage of UK mains electricity?
A: 50 Hz and about 230 V
Q: How does the potential difference across each branch of a parallel circuit compare?
A: It is the same across each branch
Tier: 3
Q: A current of 2 A flows through a 6 Ω resistor. What is the potential difference?
A: 12 V
Q: Why does the National Grid use step-up transformers?
A: To increase the potential difference so the current is smaller, which reduces energy lost heating the cables

Subject: Physics
Topic: Particle model of matter
Tier: 1
Q: What is the change of state from gas to liquid called?
A: Condensation (condensing)
Tier: 2
Q: Give the equation for density.
A: Density = mass ÷ volume
Q: What is the density of a 2 kg object with a volume of 0.5 m³?
A: 4 kg/m³
Q: What is specific latent heat?
A: The energy needed to change the state of 1 kg of a substance with no change in temperature
Tier: 3
Q: What is internal energy?
A: The total kinetic energy and potential energy of all the particles in a system
Q: A gas is heated in a sealed container. Why does its pressure rise?
A: The particles move faster, so they hit the walls more often and with more force

Subject: Physics
Topic: Atomic structure
Tier: 1
Q: Which type of nuclear radiation is the most penetrating?
A: Gamma
Q: Which type of radiation is stopped by a sheet of paper?
A: Alpha
Tier: 2
Q: What is an alpha particle made of?
A: Two protons and two neutrons (a helium nucleus)
Q: Which type of nuclear radiation is the most ionising?
A: Alpha
Q: What is half-life?
A: The time it takes for half the unstable nuclei in a sample to decay (or for the count rate to halve)
Q: What is a beta particle?
A: A high-speed electron ejected from the nucleus
Tier: 3
Q: What is the difference between irradiation and contamination?
A: Irradiation is exposure to radiation (the object does not become radioactive). Contamination is radioactive atoms getting onto or into an object
Q: What did the alpha particle scattering experiment show?
A: The mass of an atom is concentrated in a tiny, positively charged nucleus, and most of the atom is empty space

Subject: Physics
Topic: Forces
Tier: 1
Q: Give one factor that increases braking distance.
A: Any one: higher speed, wet or icy road, worn brakes, worn tyres, greater mass
Q: Stopping distance is the sum of which two distances?
A: Thinking distance and braking distance
Tier: 2
Q: Give the equation for Newton's second law.
A: Force = mass × acceleration
Q: Give the equation for work done.
A: Work done = force × distance moved along the line of action of the force
Q: Give the equation for momentum.
A: Momentum = mass × velocity
Q: What is the resultant force on an object moving at a constant velocity?
A: Zero
Tier: 3
Q: What is the weight of a 5 kg mass? (g = 9.8 N/kg)
A: 49 N
Q: What is the difference between a vector and a scalar?
A: A vector has magnitude and direction; a scalar has magnitude only
Q: A force of 2 N stretches a spring by 0.1 m. What is the spring constant?
A: 20 N/m
Q: A car speeds up from 0 to 20 m/s in 5 s. What is its acceleration?
A: 4 m/s²

Subject: Physics
Topic: Waves
Tier: 1
Q: Which electromagnetic wave has the longest wavelength?
A: Radio waves
Q: Give one use of microwaves.
A: Any one: satellite communications, cooking food
Q: Which part of the electromagnetic spectrum causes sunburn and can lead to skin cancer?
A: Ultraviolet
Tier: 2
Q: Give the wave speed equation.
A: Wave speed = frequency × wavelength
Q: Is sound a transverse or longitudinal wave?
A: Longitudinal
Q: Do all electromagnetic waves travel at the same speed in a vacuum?
A: Yes
Q: What is the amplitude of a wave?
A: The maximum displacement of a point on the wave from its undisturbed position
Tier: 3
Q: A sound wave has a speed of 340 m/s and a wavelength of 2 m. What is its frequency?
A: 170 Hz

Subject: Physics
Topic: Magnetism and electromagnetism
Tier: 1
Q: Which way do magnetic field lines point?
A: From the north pole to the south pole
Q: Name the four magnetic materials.
A: Iron, steel, cobalt and nickel
Tier: 2
Q: Give two ways to make an electromagnet stronger.
A: Any two: more turns on the coil, a bigger current, add an iron core
Q: What is the motor effect?
A: A wire carrying a current in a magnetic field experiences a force
Q: What is a solenoid?
A: A coil of wire. When a current flows it makes a magnetic field, which is strong and uniform inside the coil
Q: What is the difference between a permanent magnet and an induced magnet?
A: A permanent magnet makes its own magnetic field. An induced magnet only becomes a magnet when it is placed in a magnetic field
Tier: 3
Q: Give the equation for the force on a wire in a magnetic field.
A: Force = magnetic flux density × current × length (F = BIl)`;

CGB.PACKS.homeostasis = `Subject: Biology
Topic: Homeostasis basics
Tier: 1
Q: What is normal core body temperature?
A: 37°C
Q: Homeostasis responds to changes from where?
A: Inside and outside the body (internal and external changes)
Tier: 2
Q: Name the three conditions controlled by homeostasis.
A: Body temperature, blood glucose concentration, water levels
Q: What is homeostasis?
A: The regulation of internal conditions to maintain optimum conditions for enzyme action and all cell functions
Tier: 3
Q: Why must body temperature be kept close to 37°C?
A: So enzymes stay at their optimum and don't denature
Q: What happens to an enzyme's active site if it gets too hot?
A: It changes shape (the enzyme denatures), so the substrate no longer fits

Subject: Biology
Topic: Control systems
Tier: 1
Q: What is a stimulus?
A: A change in the environment
Q: What detects a stimulus?
A: Receptors
Q: What do effectors do?
A: Bring about a response
Q: What are the two types of effector?
A: Muscles and glands
Q: What do glands secrete?
A: Hormones
Tier: 2
Q: What does a coordination centre do?
A: Receives and processes information from receptors
Q: On a cold day, what effector warms the body up by shivering?
A: Muscles
Q: Name three coordination centres.
A: Brain, spinal cord, pancreas
Q: Put these in order: effector, stimulus, response, receptor, coordination centre.
A: Stimulus, receptor, coordination centre, effector, response
Q: What is negative feedback?
A: When a change is reversed to bring a level back towards its set point
Tier: 3
Q: In a central heating system, what acts as the effector?
A: The boiler and radiators
Q: In central heating, what acts as the coordination centre?
A: The thermostat control unit

Subject: Biology
Topic: Nervous vs hormonal
Tier: 1
Q: Which is faster: nervous or hormonal?
A: Nervous
Q: Pulling your hand off a hot pan: nervous or hormonal?
A: Nervous
Q: What type of signal does the nervous system use?
A: Electrical impulses
Q: What type of signal does the endocrine system use?
A: Hormones (chemicals)
Q: How do hormones travel around the body?
A: In the bloodstream
Tier: 2
Q: Growing taller over several years: nervous or hormonal?
A: Hormonal
Q: Which has longer-lasting effects: nervous or hormonal?
A: Hormonal
Q: Which system acts on a very precise area?
A: Nervous
Tier: 3
Q: Give two differences between nervous and hormonal responses.
A: Any two: nervous responses are faster; nervous responses are shorter-lasting; nervous responses act on a precise area; nerves use electrical impulses while hormones are chemicals carried in the blood

Subject: Biology
Topic: Nervous system
Tier: 1
Q: What does CNS stand for?
A: Central nervous system
Q: What travels along a neurone?
A: An electrical impulse
Q: True or false: effectors can be muscles or glands.
A: True
Tier: 2
Q: Which two structures make up the CNS?
A: Brain and spinal cord
Q: Name the three types of neurone.
A: Sensory, relay, motor
Q: Which neurone carries impulses from receptors to the CNS?
A: Sensory neurone
Q: Which neurone carries impulses from the CNS to effectors?
A: Motor neurone
Q: Which neurone carries impulses from sensory to motor neurones?
A: Relay neurone
Q: Where are relay neurones found?
A: In the CNS
Q: True or false: motor neurones carry impulses to the CNS.
A: False. They carry impulses from the CNS to effectors
Tier: 3
Q: A person's spinal cord is damaged in the neck. Why might they feel nothing below the injury?
A: Impulses from receptors below the injury can't travel up the spinal cord to the brain

Subject: Biology
Topic: Synapses
Tier: 1
Q: What is a synapse?
A: The junction between two neurones
Q: What crosses a synapse?
A: A chemical
Tier: 2
Q: By what process does the chemical cross the synapse?
A: Diffusion
Q: What does the chemical bind to on the next neurone?
A: Receptors
Q: True or false: the impulse jumps across the synapse as electricity.
A: False. A chemical diffuses across the gap
Q: What happens once the chemical binds to the next neurone?
A: A new electrical impulse is set up
Tier: 3
Q: Why does a pathway with more synapses take longer?
A: Diffusion of the chemical is slower than an electrical impulse along a neurone

Subject: Biology
Topic: Reflex arc
Tier: 1
Q: Is the conscious part of the brain involved in a reflex?
A: No
Q: What is the purpose of a reflex?
A: To protect the body from harm
Q: In the hot pan reflex, what is the stimulus?
A: Heat
Q: Give the reflex arc pathway from stimulus to response.
A: Stimulus, receptor, sensory neurone, relay neurone, motor neurone, effector, response
Tier: 2
Q: In the hot pan reflex, where is the receptor?
A: In the skin
Q: In the hot pan reflex, what is the effector?
A: A muscle in the arm
Q: Give the three features of a reflex.
A: Automatic, rapid, protective
Q: In the hot pan reflex, where is the relay neurone?
A: In the spinal cord
Q: Name two reflexes in the human body.
A: Any two: pupil narrowing in bright light, knee jerk, blinking, coughing, sneezing
Tier: 3
Q: How many synapses are in the hot pan reflex arc?
A: Two (sensory to relay, relay to motor)
Q: Why are reflexes so fast?
A: They bypass the conscious brain, so there is no decision-making, and the pathway is short with few synapses

Subject: Biology
Topic: Reaction time practical
Tier: 1
Q: The ruler falls a shorter distance. Is the reaction faster or slower?
A: Faster
Tier: 2
Q: If you test the effect of caffeine, what is the independent variable?
A: Caffeine (amount, or yes/no)
Q: Why must you always use the same hand?
A: The dominant hand is usually faster
Q: In the ruler drop test, what is the dependent variable?
A: Reaction time (or the distance the ruler falls)
Q: Give two control variables in the ruler drop test.
A: Any two: same hand, same ruler, same person dropping, same start position, arm resting on the table
Tier: 3
Q: Why repeat the ruler drop test five times?
A: To calculate a mean, reduce the effect of random error and spot anomalies
Q: Is catching a dropped ruler a reflex?
A: No. It is a voluntary response involving the conscious brain, so it is slower than a reflex`;
